---
import StandardPageLayout from "../../layouts/StandardPageLayout.astro";
import FinancialStatementKey from "../../components/FinancialStatementKey.astro";
import Glossary from "../../components/Glossary.astro";
import PageToc from "../../components/PageToc.astro";
import "../../styles/standards-poster.css";
import "../../styles/extended.css";
import HistoryTimeline from "../../components/HistoryTimeline.astro";
import history from "../../data/history.json";
import metadata from "../../data/standards/usgaap/asc908.json";
---

<StandardPageLayout
  code={metadata.code}
  standardType={metadata.framework}
  title={metadata.title}
  description={metadata.description}
  issued={metadata.issued}
  latestAmendment={metadata.latestAmendment}
  status={metadata.status}
  summary={metadata.summary}
  bannerNotice={metadata.bannerNotice}
>
  <Fragment slot="toc">
    <PageToc
      items={[
        { id: "s1", label: "Conceptual Framework Linkage" },
        { id: "s2", label: "Scope" },
        { id: "s3", label: "Major Maintenance (Heavy Overhaul) Costs — Three Acceptable Methods" },
        { id: "s4", label: "Frequent Flyer / Mileage Programs — Governed by ASC 606" },
        { id: "s5", label: "Route Acquisition Costs and Airframe/Engine Costs" },
        { id: "s6", label: "Worked Example — Contrasting Major Maintenance Methods" },
        { id: "s7", label: "Worked Example — Frequent Flyer Program Revenue Deferral" },
        { id: "s8", label: "Disclosure Requirements" },
        { id: "s9", label: "Critical Judgments &amp; Estimates" },
        { id: "s10", label: "No Dedicated IFRS Industry Standard — Discussion" },
        { id: "s11", label: "Statement Mapping" },
        { id: "s12", label: "Related Topics" },
        { id: "s13", label: "Glossary" }
      ]}
    />
  </Fragment>
  <div class="wrap">

    <div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Conceptual Framework Linkage</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">Specialised industry practices layered on general GAAP</div><div style="font-size: var(--text-small); color: var(--color-text-secondary); line-height: 1.6;">ASC 908 doesn't establish an entirely SEPARATE accounting model for airlines — it provides INDUSTRY-SPECIFIC guidance and elaboration on how GENERAL GAAP principles (PP&amp;E capitalisation under ASC 360, revenue recognition under ASC 606) apply to practices UNIQUE to the airline industry, particularly the treatment of MAJOR MAINTENANCE (heavy overhaul) costs and FREQUENT FLYER/mileage programs.</div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>Scope</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Commercial passenger and cargo airlines</div><div style="font-size: var(--text-small); color: var(--color-text-secondary); line-height: 1.6;">ASC 908 applies to entities operating as commercial AIRLINES, addressing their aircraft maintenance capitalisation practices and customer loyalty program accounting.</div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>Major Maintenance (Heavy Overhaul) Costs — Three Acceptable Methods</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">A genuine policy choice, applied consistently by asset type</div>
    <div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2fr;"><span>Method</span><span>Description</span></div>
    <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2fr;"><span>Direct expensing</span><span>Major maintenance costs are expensed as incurred, with NO capitalisation — the simplest, most conservative approach</span></div>
    <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2fr;"><span>Built-in overhaul (component depreciation)</span><span>A portion of the aircraft's cost is separately identified as relating to the overhaul component and depreciated over the period until the NEXT scheduled overhaul; actual overhaul costs are then capitalised and depreciated over the following overhaul interval</span></div>
    <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2fr;"><span>Deferral method</span><span>Major maintenance costs are capitalised when incurred and amortised over the period until the next scheduled overhaul</span></div>
    </div>
    <div class="note">Airlines may elect any of these three methods as an accounting POLICY, applied consistently to similar assets — a genuine, sanctioned choice among methods, unlike many other Topics where a single method is mandated.</div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>Frequent Flyer / Mileage Programs — Governed by ASC 606</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">A material right, deferred as a separate performance obligation</div><div style="font-size: var(--text-small); color: var(--color-text-secondary); line-height: 1.6;">Under current guidance, frequent flyer MILEAGE credits earned on a ticket purchase are treated as a MATERIAL RIGHT under ASC 606 — a SEPARATE PERFORMANCE OBLIGATION distinct from the transportation service itself. A PORTION of the ticket transaction price is ALLOCATED to the mileage credits (based on their relative standalone selling price, factoring in expected redemption rates), DEFERRED as a contract liability, and recognised as REVENUE only when the miles are actually REDEEMED (or expire unused). This REPLACED the older "incremental cost" accrual method that predated ASC 606's material rights framework.</div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s5"><span class="num">5</span><h2>Route Acquisition Costs and Airframe/Engine Costs</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Capitalised where they represent a genuine future economic benefit</div><div style="font-size: var(--text-small); color: var(--color-text-secondary); line-height: 1.6;">Costs of ACQUIRING route authorities/airport slots may be CAPITALISED as intangible assets where they meet general recognition criteria (ASC 350), and airframe/engine costs are capitalised and depreciated under normal ASC 360 PP&amp;E principles, WITH the major maintenance policy election (Section 3) layered on top for subsequent overhaul costs.</div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s6"><span class="num">6</span><h2>Worked Example — Contrasting Major Maintenance Methods</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">The same overhaul, three different expense patterns</div>
    <div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;"><div class="wk-title">Example: A $2,000,000 engine overhaul, 5-year interval to next overhaul</div><div class="wk-body">An airline incurs a $2,000,000 major engine overhaul cost, with the next scheduled overhaul expected in 5 years.</div><div class="wk-answer"><strong>Direct expensing method:</strong> the full $2,000,000 is expensed IMMEDIATELY in the year incurred.<br><strong>Deferral method:</strong> the $2,000,000 is CAPITALISED and AMORTISED evenly over the 5-year interval — <strong>$400,000 per year</strong>.<br><strong>Built-in overhaul method:</strong> a PORTION of the aircraft's original cost (representing the PRIOR overhaul component) would have already been depreciated down to near-zero by this point; the new $2,000,000 cost is now capitalised as the NEW overhaul component and depreciated over the NEXT 5-year interval, similarly to the deferral method's pattern going forward.</div></div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s7"><span class="num">7</span><h2>Worked Example — Frequent Flyer Program Revenue Deferral</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">Allocating a portion of the ticket price to mileage credits</div>
    <div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;"><div class="wk-title">Example: A $600 ticket earning frequent flyer miles</div><div class="wk-body">A passenger purchases a $600 ticket, earning frequent flyer miles with an estimated standalone selling price (factoring in expected redemption) of $30.</div><div class="wk-answer">The $600 transaction price is ALLOCATED between the transportation service and the mileage material right based on relative standalone selling price: approximately <strong>$570 allocated to the flight</strong> (recognised as revenue when the flight occurs), and <strong>$30 allocated to the mileage credits</strong> (deferred as a contract liability, recognised as revenue only as/when the miles are later redeemed for a future flight, upgrade, or other reward, or expire unused).</div></div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s8"><span class="num">8</span><h2>Disclosure Requirements</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">What ASC 908 requires</div><div class="check-list"><span>Accounting policy for major maintenance costs (which of the three methods is used)</span><span>Nature of frequent flyer/mileage program obligations and the deferred revenue balance</span></div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s9"><span class="num">9</span><h2>Critical Judgments &amp; Estimates</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cn span2"><div class="ctitle cn">Where judgement matters most</div>
    <div class="two-col"><div><div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT JUDGEMENTS</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Selecting a major maintenance accounting method and applying it consistently</span></div></div>
    <div><div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT ESTIMATES</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Standalone selling price of mileage credits, factoring in expected redemption rates and breakage</span><span>Expected interval to the next scheduled major maintenance event</span></div></div></div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s10"><span class="num">10</span><h2>No Dedicated IFRS Industry Standard — Discussion</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">A narrower comparison — IFRS addresses these issues through general principles rather than an airline-specific standard</div><div style="font-size: var(--text-small); color: var(--color-text-secondary); line-height: 1.6;">IFRS does NOT have a DEDICATED airline industry standard paralleling ASC 908. Airlines reporting under IFRS instead apply GENERAL principles: major maintenance capitalisation decisions are made under IAS 16's general component depreciation approach (broadly similar in SPIRIT to the "built-in overhaul" method, since IAS 16 explicitly requires SIGNIFICANT PARTS of an asset with different useful lives, such as an engine overhaul component, to be depreciated separately) — while direct expensing and the deferral method as SEPARATE named alternatives are more distinctly a US GAAP construct. Frequent flyer programs were historically addressed by IFRIC 13 (Customer Loyalty Programmes), a narrower interpretation applying essentially the SAME "material right"/deferred revenue logic — IFRIC 13 has since been SUPERSEDED and folded into IFRS 15's general material rights framework, arriving at a broadly CONVERGED outcome with ASC 606 for loyalty programs specifically, even without a dedicated airline standard.</div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s11"><span class="num">11</span><h2>Statement Mapping</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">Where every ASC 908 event lands</div>
    <div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Event</span><span>Statement(s) affected</span><span>Notes</span></div>
    <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Major maintenance (deferral/built-in overhaul methods)</span><span><span class="badge bs">BS</span> <span class="badge bp">IS</span></span><span>Capitalised, then depreciated/amortised over the overhaul interval</span></div>
    <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Major maintenance (direct expensing method)</span><span><span class="badge bp">IS</span></span><span>Expensed immediately as incurred</span></div>
    <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Mileage credit allocation</span><span><span class="badge bs">BS</span> <span class="badge bp">IS</span></span><span>Deferred as a contract liability, recognised as revenue upon redemption</span></div>
    </div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s12"><span class="num">12</span><h2>Related Topics</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Cross-reference map</div>
    <div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>Topic</span><span>Connection</span></div>
    <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>ASC 360</span><span>Property, Plant &amp; Equipment — supplies the general capitalisation and depreciation framework underlying aircraft accounting</span></div>
    <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>ASC 606</span><span>Governs the material right allocation and revenue deferral mechanics for frequent flyer programs</span></div>
    </div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s13"><span class="num">13</span><h2>Quick-Reference Glossary</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cn span2">
    <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Deferral method</div><div class="glossary-def">A major maintenance accounting method capitalising overhaul costs and amortising them over the period until the next scheduled overhaul.</div></div>
    <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Built-in overhaul method</div><div class="glossary-def">A major maintenance accounting method separately depreciating an asset's overhaul component over the interval to the next scheduled overhaul.</div></div>
    <div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;"><div class="glossary-term">Material right</div><div class="glossary-def">A separate performance obligation under ASC 606 arising from a customer option (such as frequent flyer miles) that provides a discount or benefit the customer would not receive without entering the original contract.</div></div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <FinancialStatementKey />

  </div>
  <section id="history">
    <HistoryTimeline items={history.asc908 || []} />
  </section>
</StandardPageLayout>

